PMCID: PMC13366050 PMID:
42444608
  <heading level="2">Abstract</heading>
  <text><![CDATA[Virtual cells represent a promising paradigm to understand cellular mechanisms, behavior, and dynamics. The realization of virtual cells relies on the accurate modeling of cellular dynamics from large-scale, multi-modal single-cell data. However, experiment-specific technical noise and intrinsic biological heterogeneity pose major challenges for virtual cell modeling. To address this gap, we present scDifformer, a context-aware transformer model augmented with a denoising diffusion module and a dedicated post-training phase. This three-phase design, comprising masked language model pre-training, diffusion-driven post-training, and downstream fine-tuning, directly enhances scDifformer's ability to denoise sparse, noisy data and generalize across studies. Benchmarking across seven tissues and multiple independent studies shows that the diffusion module consistently improves cross-dataset performance, particularly in settings with strong batch effects. By combining the strengths of transformer and diffusion models, scDifformer achieves state-of-the-art performance in cell type annotation across diverse datasets. It further demonstrates robust capability in resolving immune cell identities across multiple tissues, accurately recovering key marker genes, functional pathways, and cross-tissue differentiation trajectories. Finally, by integrating scDifformer with a graph neural network, we extend its utility to spatial transcriptomics, significantly enhancing spot-level deconvolution accuracy. Altogether, scDifformer provides a scalable and biologically grounded framework for modeling heterogeneous single-cell data, offering a powerful foundation for the development of high-fidelity, multi-modal virtual cell models.]]></text>
  <heading level="2">Graphical Abstract</heading>
  <picture>
    <caption>For image description, please refer to the figure legend and surrounding text.</caption>
  </picture>
  <heading level="2">Introduction</heading>
The concept of a virtual cell was proposed decades ago as a framework to understand cell behavior and transitions over time [
1
]. It is a computational representation that captures the dynamic interactions and regulatory processes of all DNA, RNA, proteins, and metabolites within a cell. Early efforts toward building virtual cells relied on systems of ordinary differential equations to model cellular networks [
2
]. However, the inherent complexity of intracellular processes and the lack of high-resolution molecular data limited the feasibility of these approaches. The advent of next-generation sequencing technologies, such as single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) and spatial transcriptomics (ST), has revolutionized our ability to profile cells across tissues, conditions, and developmental stages. These technologies have generated massive, high-dimensional datasets that capture cellular heterogeneity and dynamics with unprecedented resolution [
3
]. Concurrently, advances in deep learning, particularly natural language models [
4
], have enabled the development of scalable, data-driven models that can extract meaningful biological representations from these complex datasets. Together, these innovations have laid the foundation for constructing virtual cell models that are both comprehensive and generalizable.
A major recent direction in this effort involves the development of cell foundation models, which are large-scale pretrained models designed to learn universal representations of single-cell transcriptomes. Most existing models adopt transformer-based architectures, inspired by advances in natural language processing (NLP) and biological sequence modeling, whereas Saturn [
5
] and SCimilarity [
6
] employ alternative architecture. Geneformer [
7
], GeneCompass [
8
], and scBERT [
9
] leverage masked language modeling (MLM) on rank- or token-based gene expression encodings to learn contextual embeddings. These models show strong performance on tasks such as cell type classification, though they often rely heavily on input normalization schemes to control for batch effects and sparsity. Other approaches emphasize architectural advances. Models such as scGPT [
10
], CellFM [
11
], scFoundation [
12
], and xTrimoGene [
13
] treat full expression profiles as sparse sequences, using attention mechanisms or state-space models to capture complex gene-cell dependencies. These architectures support a wide range of downstream applications, from transfer learning to perturbation response prediction, and can be trained at a massive scale. A third emerging direction focuses on cross-species generalization, with models like Universal Cell Embedding [
14
] and TranscriptFormer [
15
] aiming to learn foundational representations that transfer across species boundaries, an essential step toward virtual cell modeling in evolutionary and comparative biology.
Despite these advances, several challenges remain. First, current models often struggle to fully denoise technical noise, such as batch effects introduced by different platforms, protocols, or labs. Most efforts to address this rely on input-level preprocessing rather than integrating noise-aware mechanisms within model architectures. Second, while diffusion models have shown impressive denoising capabilities in protein design [
16
], they are rarely adopted in single-cell transcriptomics, despite their natural advantage in mitigating noise and heterogeneity that are pervasive in single-cell data. Third, relatively few foundation models are equipped to integrate multi-modal data, such as jointly modeling scRNA-seq and spatial transcriptomics, limiting their utility in reconstructing tissue-level organization and dynamics.
  <text>To address these challenges, we present scDifformer, a diffusion-augmented transformer model enhanced with a diffusion-driven post-training phase designed to improve the fidelity and generalizability of virtual cell representations. Trained on a corpus of 34.2 million human single-cell transcriptomes spanning diverse tissues and biological conditions, scDifformer captures rich context-aware gene expression patterns while actively denoising technical variation through a discrete diffusion module. A second training stage, absent in most existing frameworks, enables the model to recover clean, biologically meaningful signals from sparse and heterogeneous data. In addition to accurate cell type annotation, scDifformer also enables the robust integration of immune cells from multiple organs and accurately captures gene- and pathway-level differences. Notably, applied to real-world data, it reconstructs canonical hematopoietic trajectories from hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells (HSPCs) to terminal immune lineages across diverse tissues, including bone marrow, spleen, thymus, and peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs). We further demonstrate its potential usage in spatial transcriptomic data analysis, by integrating it with a graph neural network, GraphSAGE, to perform spot-level deconvolution. This approach offers a promising direction for unifying single-cell and spatial information within a shared modeling framework. These results highlight scDifformer as a scalable, biologically grounded framework capable of unifying single-cell information, and advancing the construction of high-fidelity, multi-modal virtual cell models.</text>
  <heading level="2">Materials and methods</heading>
  <heading level="3">Standardizing pre-training corpus for accurate transcriptomic profiling</heading>
To create a comprehensive pre-trained corpus, we included 36.77 million human single-cell transcriptomes from various publicly available datasets. We focused on droplet-based sequencing techniques to ensure consistency in expression value metrics. We filtered potential doublets and retained cells with both total reads and mitochondrial reads laying within a 3-standard-deviation margin of the average values for each measurement within that particular dataset. Cells with elevated mutational loads were carefully excluded to maintain data integrity [
17
] and prevent potential misinterpretations arising from substantial network rewiring that can not be detected by genome sequencing [
18
]. Only Ensembl-annotated protein-coding/microRNA genes were kept for downstream processing. Cells expressing fewer than seven detected genes were excluded from the analysis since the 15% masking strategy used during pre-training was unable to effectively obscure individual genes in cells with reduced transcriptional detection [
19
]. Ultimately, a total of 34.2 million cells from 7225 samples met the defined filtration criteria and were included for further analysis. These cells were compiled and stored in .loom HDF5 format, accompanied by metadata extracted from the original studies.
To represent the transcriptome of each single cell non-parametrically, we followed instructions of the rank value encoding method [
7
]. Briefly, this method prioritized genes distinguishing cellular states via ratings normalized throughout the corpus, thereby highlighting transcription factors expressed at lower levels while attenuating genes that are universally expressed. The specific steps involved calculating the nonzero median expression for each detected gene across all cells and then normalizing the gene expression against total read counts of the sample and its corresponding nonzero median. This yielded the normalized expression values. Finally, we ranked the normalized expression values of each cell to obtain rank values. Tokenization of encodings made use of a 23 698-gene vocabulary. The Huggingface Datasets, which offers computational efficiency and facilitates archiving, was utilized to store our data.
  <heading level="3">Three-stage training paradigm of scDifformer</heading>
<![CDATA[Unlike conventional pre-training approaches founded upon a two-phase architecture consisting of "pre-training" and "fine-tuning" stages, the proposed scDifformer subjects the pre-trained weights to continued post-training using diffusion probabilistic models, rather than directly fine-tuning the pre-trained model after the initial pre-training phase. This additional post-training serves to further reduce noise and refine the learned representations by allowing for incremental parameter updates within the scDifformer framework. The integrated scDifformer architecture depicted in Fig.]]>  
1
achieves representational enhancement and learns richer contextualized embeddings for downstream scRNA-seq analysis tasks.
  <picture>
    <caption>scDifformer architecture and tissue distribution of the training dataset. ( A ) Overview of the three-stage training framework of scDifformer, including pre-training, post-training, and fine-tuning. The model introduces a diffusion-based post-training stage following conventional MLM pre-training to refine transformer representations. This stage integrates the advantages of both transformer and diffusion models, enhancing knowledge acquisition and accelerating convergence. ( B ) Tissue composition of the scRNA-seq dataset used for pre- and post-training, comprising over 34 million cells across diverse human tissues.</caption>
  </picture>
  <heading level="3">scDifformer architecture and pre-training</heading>
  <text>In the pre-training stage, we constructed a transformer encoder to leverage the multi-head self-attention mechanism to learn relationships between genes within single-cell transcriptomic data. Specifically, it consists of the following key components: gene embedding layer that maps each gene to a low-dimensional vector as the input, and a transformer encoder containing multiple self-attention and feedforward blocks to capture contextual information. During the pre-training process, 15% of genes within each transcriptome were randomly masked. The objective of pre-training was to train the model to infer the identity of genes occupying the masked positions by utilizing the contextual information provided by the unmasked genes, thereby enabling it to accurately represent the gene expression patterns of the respective cell states.</text>
We trained our own model from scratch based on partially the code of Geneformer [
7
] using over 34 million single-cell transcriptomic profiles collected in-house. Our model architecture utilized 24 layers of transformer encoder units, representing, to our knowledge, the deepest pre-trained single-cell model to date, termed transformer-24L. Other parameters of the model include an input size of 2048, an embedding dimension of 512, and eight attention heads per layer. This model was pre-trained for 10 epochs to ensure thorough optimization of parameters. Subsequent empirical analysis validated that this configuration of model depth and pre-training duration achieved superior performance. The pre-training procedure aimed to identify an optimal hyperparameter configuration, including a maximum learning rate of 2 × 10
  <superscript>−4</superscript>
, linear learning rate scheduling with warmup, the Adam optimizer with weight decay regularization [
20
], warmup steps of 20 000, L2 weight decay of 0.001, and a batch size of 6. This pre-training setup allowed for effective representation learning from our considerable single-cell transcriptomics dataset [
19
].
To pre-train efficiently on sizable datasets, we operationalized the most recent innovations in distributed graphics processing unit (GPU) training using Deepspeed [
21
-
23
]. This encompassed parcelling model parameters, gradients, and optimizer states crosswise GPUs to maximize computing power utilization and refine effectiveness. It also involved redistributing as much handling and memory as feasible to central processing units to adapt to GPUs, decreasing GPU memory usage and advancing scale and proficiency. Memory fragmentation was decreased by discriminating long-term and short-term allocations, boosting utilization and performance [
19
].
  <text>Through massive self-supervised pre-training on single-cell datasets, transformer-24L learned the complex interaction patterns between genes. The pre-trained transformer-24L can be directly used for downstream single-cell analysis or applied to other omics data like proteomics and multicellular contexts. The pre-training stage was conducted on 8 computing nodes using a total of 64 NVIDIA A100 GPUs for 10 epochs, requiring ~119 h.</text>
  <heading level="3">Post-training of scDifformer</heading>
<![CDATA[We proposed scDifformer with a novel "post-training" phase for post-training transformer-24L with transcriptomic data, inspired by DiffusionBERT from NLP field []]>  
24
], aiming to improve masked language models using diffusion models in the single-cell area. We chose diffusion models because they have been proven to achieve a superior trade-off between generation quality and diversity with its training strategy [
24
]. The high quality of generation corresponds to the high accuracy of data prediction, and its diversity advantage aligns with the inherent diversity of cell types in transcriptomic data.
<![CDATA[Different from the existing single-cell large models' paradigm that mainly depends only on language understanding models, the generative language models we used are complementary to language understanding models. Diffusion models include two processes: a forward process that gradually adds noise to the data, and a reverse process that denoises the data. During training, both diffusion models and transformer-based pre-trained language models share a learning objective, which involves the task of noise reduction []]>  
24
]. This allows scDifformer to leverage the advantages of both models. Pre-trained denoising language models like transformer-24L provide a good initialization that accelerates the convergence of the training process in the diffusion model. On the other side, scDifformer post-trains transformer-24L to learn the reverse process of a discrete diffusion process with an absorbing state to help improve the quality of data generation [
24
].
To facilitate the forward diffusion process, scDifformer utilizes the spindle noise schedule, which was originally derived from text generation tasks [
24
]. This schedule regulates the amount of noise introduced at each step, taking into account the information contained in each token. Specifically, its purpose is to ensure that the most informative token is given priority for masking in the initial stages, allowing the model to learn to recover it in the final position. This approach aligns with the easy-first generation hypothesis prevalent in denoising language models [
24
,
25
].
<![CDATA[As to the matter of temporal encoding schemes, a typical diffusion model's reverse process predicts the denoised sample by taking a corrupted input sample and time step as inputs, represented as , where the time step]]>  
  <italic>t</italic>
is an exogenous variable not observed during the pre-training of language models. In our approach, we do not explicitly include the time step
  <italic>t</italic>
but infer it from the noised sample , motivated by time-agnostic decoding (TAD) within the scope of NLP [
24
]. Unlike images, due to the discrete nature of rank-encoded transcriptomic tokens, the diffusion time step can be implicitly deduced in sequences by counting masked tokens. This allows the pre-trained model to perform iterative decoding without representing the current time step, i.e. . Through time step inference rather than explicit modeling, this method facilitates the direct adaptation of diffusion models to the parameters of the base pre-trained model, i.e. transformer-24L, specifically in terms of time step incorporation, without modifications to the embedding structure or model architecture, accordingly improving the training efficiency.
  <picture>
    <caption>Inline graphic</caption>
  </picture>
  <picture>
    <caption>Inline graphic</caption>
  </picture>
  <picture>
    <caption>Inline graphic</caption>
  </picture>
  <text><![CDATA[In summary, the diffusion post-training phase is theoretically a process that leverages the pre-trained transformer as a denoising function and continuously optimizes its weights by minimizing the variational lower bound of a discrete diffusion process. It does not alter the model architecture but refines and enhances the model's already learned representations through a new generative training objective (from noise to data) that is compatible with the MLM objective, making it more adept at recovering clear, generalizable biological signals from complex, heterogeneous real-world single-cell data. The diffusion-based post-training stage was performed on 9 computing nodes with 72 NVIDIA A100 GPUs for 3 epochs, requiring ~65.6 h.]]></text>
  <text>Experimental results on downstream tasks demonstrate that scDifformer not only achieved significant improvement over transformer-24L but also had superior performance compared to existing single-cell pre-training models in downstream tasks.</text>
  <heading level="3">scDifformer gene embeddings, cell embeddings, and attention weights</heading>
There are mainly two strategies to apply pre-trained language representations to downstream tasks: feature-based and fine-tuning. The feature-based method establishes an architecture specific to the task to incorporate pre-trained representations as additional features [
26
]. Extractable features in scDifformer include gene embeddings, cell embeddings, and attention weights. When each single-cell transcriptomic data are input into the scDifformer model, the model projects each gene into a 512-dimensional vectorized semantic space. Thus, the gene embedding can be extracted from the hidden state weights of the model. In particular, it can be extracted from the penultimate layer, because the output layer is more closely related to the predictive task, while the penultimate layer provides a more generalizable representation [
19
<![CDATA[]. scDifformer's cellular embeddings can be generated by averaging the embeddings of all detected genes within that cell, likewise yielding a 512-dimensional vector.]]>  
  <text>Each scDifformer layer comprises eight attention heads, intending to learn different classes of genes and their relationships. The attention weights represent the contextual dependency or association strength from one gene to another, as learned by the model, and are interpreted as indicative of gene-gene interactions. We referred to the scBERT method for extracting the attention weights, which are computed during the self-attention operation and can be obtained for each attention head. There are 24 layers with eight attention heads in each layer of scDifformer. We chose to use the last layer to extract attention weights of our model. For integration, we take an element-wise average across all attention metrics of the eight attention heads in the last layer. In this average attention matrix, each value indicates the amount of attention paid from gene to gene . To emphasize the significance of genes to each cell, we summed the attention matrix column-wise to obtain an attention-sum vector, which has a length equal to the number of input genes.</text>
  <picture>
    <caption>Inline graphic</caption>
