  </picture>
  <heading level="3">scDifformer fine-tuning</heading>
  <text>Fine-tuning was implemented by initializing the model with post-trained weights and adding a final task-specific layer. In this study, we focused on the downstream task of cell-type annotation, which is a classification task. Specifically, a classification head consisting of a linear layer on top of the pooled output was added on top of the scDifformer model. The purpose of this head is to map the final output of the scDifformer model to the appropriate label i.e. cell-type for the classification task. The parameters of the model were updated during training on this specific task in the fine-tuning stage. Fine-tuning results for cell-type categorization were reported as accuracy and F1 score utilizing five-fold cross-validation, training on 80% labels and testing the remaining 20% held-out labels across five iterations. Furthermore, cell type fine-tuning was additionally assessed in an inter-dataset setting to evaluate the robustness and cross-dataset generalization performance of the model.</text>
  <heading level="3">Datasets used in benchmarking</heading>
  <heading level="4">Intra-dataset classification</heading>
Five datasets were used for intra-dataset evaluation under a cross-validation scheme [
27
]. The Zheng68K PBMC dataset [
28
] consists of 68 579 cells and 11 cell types. It was acquired from
https://www.10xgenomics.com/resources/datasets/fresh-68-k-pbm-cs-donor-a-1-standard-1-1-0
. And the annotation of each barcode is available at
https://github.com/10XGenomics/single-cell-3prime-paper
. The lung dataset [
29
] was acquired from
https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn21041850
. The aligned pancreas dataset was a combination of Baron [
30
] (
GSE84133
), Enge [
31
] (
GSE81547
), Muraro [
32
] (
GSE85241
), and Segerstolpe [
33
] (E-MTAB-5061). The kidney [
34
] and liver [
35
] datasets used in intra-dataset classification are available in Gene Expression Omnibus under
GSE115469
and
GSE114530
, respectively.
  <heading level="4">Inter-dataset classification</heading>
We collected five paired datasets, including pancreas (Baron [
30
]:
GSE84133
and Enge [
31
]:
GSE81547
), kidney (Lake [
36
]:
GSE121862
and Muto [
37
]:
GSE151302
), lung (Travaglini [
29
]:
https://www.synapse.org/#!Synapse:syn21041850
and Madissoon [
38
]: PRJEB31843), PBMC [
39
] (Single Cell Portal accession numbers SCP424), and liver (MacParland [
35
]:
GSE115469
and Aizarani [
40
]:
GSE124395
) for inter-dataset evaluation. Each paired dataset contained two datasets from the same tissue but different studies. Each of them was used once as training data and once as test data. Related annotations were obtained from Cell BLAST database [
41
].
  <heading level="4"><![CDATA[Simulating "multi-cell-spot" datasets]]></heading>
In order to benchmark the deconvolution softwares, we simulated spots containing multiple cells with known cell composition within each spot as introduced in [
42
]. Mouse visual cortex data [
43
<![CDATA[] was gridded into 253 squares (600 × 600 pixels each) according to the coordinates of each cell. Only squares with more than two cells were kept in the following benchmarking. The center of each square was considered as the coordinate of the "spot." The sum of read count of each cell was considered as the total read count of the spot. We take the percentage of each cell type in each spot as the ground truth. Single-cell RNA-seq data of mouse visual cortex served as reference for deconvolution was acquired from []]>  
44
].
  <heading level="3">Comparison methods</heading>
  <text>We benchmarked eight scRNA-seq cell annotation methods and eight deconvolution methods for spatial transcriptomics.</text>
  <heading level="4">scRNA-seq cell annotation methods</heading>
  <bold>Geneformer</bold>
: The code is available on Geneformer website (
https://huggingface.co/ctheodoris/Geneformer/blob/main/examples/cell_classification.ipynb
). We used the default parameters.
  <bold>scBERT</bold>
: The code is available on GitHub repository:
https://github.com/TencentAILabHealthcare/scBERT
. We used the default parameters.
  <bold>scmap</bold>
: We followed the tutorial from Bioconductor:
https://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/vignettes/scmap/inst/doc/scmap.html
. Parameters for scmap-cluster and scmap-cell were set as: scmap-Cluster: projection = NULL, index_list = NULL, threshold = 0.4). scmap-cell: scmapCell_results = NULL, cluster_list = NULL, w = 3, threshold = 0.4.
  <bold>CHETAH</bold>
: We followed the tutorial from Bioconductor:
https://www.bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/vignettes/CHETAH/inst/doc/CHETAH_introduction.html
and used default parameters.
  <bold>scPred</bold>
: We followed the tutorial from
https://powellgenomicslab.github.io/scPred/articles/introduction.html
<![CDATA[. We set the model as "glm".]]>  
  <bold>scClassify</bold>
: We used the guidelines on GitHub repository (
https://sydneybiox.github.io/scClassify/
<![CDATA[). Pre-trained model was obtained with parameters selectFeatures = c("limma", "BI"), The trained model was obtained with parameters prob_threshold = 0.7.]]>  
  <bold>Garnett</bold>
: We followed the tutorial from
https://cole-trapnell-lab.github.io/garnett
. Marker genes of each dataset were selected using FindAllMarkers function in Seurat.
  <bold>scFoundation</bold>
: We followed the tutorial from
https://github.com/biomap-research/scFoundation
.
  <bold>CellFM</bold>
: We followed the tutorial from
https://github.com/biomed-AI/CellFM
.
  <bold>scGPT</bold>
: We followed the tutorial from
https://github.com/bowang-lab/scgpt
.
  <heading level="4">Spatial transcriptomics deconvolution methods</heading>
  <bold>Cell2location</bold>
: We followed the tutorial from
https://cell2location.readthedocs.io/en/latest/notebooks/cell2location_tutorial.html
. The cell2location model was obtained with parameters max_epochs = 30 000.
  <bold>Tangram</bold>
: We used the guidelines on GitHub repository (
https://github.com/broadinstitute/Tangram
<![CDATA[). We set the parameter as mode='clusters'.]]>  
  <bold>RCTD</bold>
: We followed the tutorial from the website (
https://raw.githack.com/dmcable/spacexr/master/vignettes/spatial-transcriptomics.html
<![CDATA[). We set the doublet mode parameter as "doublet".]]>  
  <bold>SpatialDWLS</bold>
: We used the guidelines on GitHub repository (
https://github.com/rdong08/spatialDWLS_dataset
). We set parameters as dimensions_to_use = 1:10, n_cell = 40.
  <bold>Stride</bold>
: We used the tutorial on the website (
https://stridespatial.readthedocs.io/en/latest/tutorials/Mouse_embryo.html
<![CDATA[). We added the parameter "-normalize" when deconvolution.]]>  
  <bold>stdGCN</bold>
: We followed the tutorial on the GitHub repository (
https://github.com/luoyuanlab/stdgcn/blob/main/Tutorial.ipynb
). Adjusted
  <italic>P</italic>
-value threshold was set to .1.
  <bold>DSTG</bold>
: We followed the tutorial on the GitHub repository (
https://github.com/Su-informatics-lab/DSTG
). Default parameters were used.
  <bold>scGPT-spatial</bold>
: We followed the tutorial on the GitHub repository (
https://github.com/bowang-lab/scGPT-spatial
). Default parameters were used.
  <heading level="3">Application of model in spatial transcriptome deconvolution</heading>
  <text>scDifformer cell embeddings can encode characteristics of the state of that single cell. We tried to utilize the diffusion embeddings to enhance the cell information so as to improve deconvolution performance. For each cell, the embeddings generated at first would be the shape of [2048, 512], which represents the dimension of tokens and the dimension of gene embeddings. Since the size for the embedding is too large, we first straighten the embedding matrix and then used Principal Component Analysis (PCA) to reduce the dimension from 2048*512 to 1024. We used the second-to-last layer of the models to extract our embeddings, as the final layer is known to capture features more directly related to the learning objective prediction, while the second-to-last layer provides a more generalizable representation.</text>
The embeddings generated by the diffusion model were utilized in deconvolution of ST data. We first embedded the raw scRNA-seq data and the ST data separately using scDifformer, following the procedure above. For embeddings of the reference scRNA-data, pseudo-spots were generated by randomly selecting a mixture of cells. Here, we used a sampling-by-cell-type method [
45
] to obtain a more balanced pseudo-spot generation (
Supplementary Tables S7
and
S8
). The pseudo-spots generated by scRNA data and ST embedding were combined. Meanwhile, we identified cell-type marker genes from the raw expression matrix using logistic regression. The expression matrix of these marker genes was finally concatenated with the combined embeddings.
We constructed the
  <italic>k</italic>
-nearest neighbouring graph based on the above pseudo- and real spots and finally fed it into an inductive graph neural network, GraphSAGE [
46
], to learn the information from the neighbouring spots. The graph of our GraphSAGE model contains all pseudo-spots and real-spots as nodes so that the real spots can learn information and can be adapted from pseudo-spots. Our model is to predict the cell-type proportions of the real spots. The label is , where and represents the spot number of real- and pseudo-spots and represents the number of cell types. And the input feature of our model contains two matrices: expression matrix ( ) and embedding matrix ( ), where
  <italic>u</italic>
and
  <italic>v</italic>
devote the dimensions of marker genes and scDifformer embeddings. The final input of the model will be the concatenated matrix ( ). And each spot input will be sent into the GraphSAGE module to update. The output of GraphSAGE module would be , which will be the input feature of two other two-layer fully connected neural networks with the function:
