  <heading level="3">Enrichment analysis</heading>
Cell-type enrichment analysis of top 100 attention genes was performed by WebCSEA (Web-based Cell-type Specific Enrichment Analysis of Genes) [
51
]. We performed the GO analyses by Metascape [
52
] with default parameters (
  <italic>P</italic>
<![CDATA[< .01, min overlap = 3 and min enrichment = 1.5).]]>  
  <heading level="3">Pseudotime trajectory inference</heading>
  <text>To evaluate the performance of scDifformer and conventional scVI embeddings in pseudotime trajectory inference, we collected single-cell RNA-seq data from thymus, bone marrow, PBMC, and spleen via the cellxgene platform. The raw count matrices from these four tissues were merged into a single dataset to serve as a testbed for downstream analysis. We first assessed the distribution of annotated cell types and visualized potential batch effects in the unintegrated data. Subsequently, we generated low-dimensional embeddings using both scVI and scDifformer. For each embedding strategy, we performed pseudotime trajectory inference using the TrajInfer module in the omicverse framework, allowing visualization of developmental lineages across diverse immune cell populations.</text>
  <heading level="2">Results</heading>
  <heading level="3">scDifformer model architecture and pre- and post-training strategies</heading>
Understanding cellular behavior through virtual cell models requires learning from increasingly large and complex single-cell transcriptomic data. However, technical noise and biological heterogeneity pose major challenges to building accurate and generalizable representations. Existing foundation models often rely on transformer architectures and two-stage training (pre-training and fine-tuning), which can struggle with noisy or sparse signals. To address this, we introduce scDifformer, a three-stage framework that incorporates a diffusion-based post-training module to denoise embeddings and improve robustness across diverse biological contexts (Fig.
1A
).
  <heading level="4">Construction of the training corpus</heading>
To construct a comprehensive and high-quality corpus for pre-training, we compiled a total of 34.2 million single cells derived from over 7000 publicly available studies, integrating data from large-scale repositories such as DISCO [
53
] and CZ CELLxGENE [
54
], representing cells from diversity tissues (Fig.
1B
<![CDATA[). To ensure consistency and reliability across datasets, we implemented a standardized filtering pipeline (see "Materials and methods" section). To represent the transcriptome of each cell in a way that is compatible with transformer architectures, we adopted the rank value encoding strategy, which encodes the relative expression ranks of genes rather than their absolute values (Fig.]]>  
1A
). As previously demonstrated in Geneformer [
7
], rank-based encoding effectively transforms transcriptomic profiles into a language-like input sequence, enabling the model to capture contextual dependencies among genes while preserving biologically meaningful expression pattern. This encoding is well-suited to transformer architectures, as it preserves co-expression pattern while reducing sensitivity to technical variation.
  <heading level="4">Self-supervised transformer pre-training</heading>
During the pre-training phase, we adopted a transformer encoder architecture [
55
] to process the rank-encoded transcriptomic inputs. Several prior implementations have successfully combined transformer-based models with single-cell transcriptomics or gene expression data [
7
,
14
]. Rather than reinventing the architecture, we built upon existing advances, particularly Geneformer, a single-cell foundation model that incorporates attention mechanisms and contextual modeling. Scdifformers also leverages self-attention to identify salient genes from high-dimensional transcriptomic inputs, optimizing predictive performance for cell-level inference tasks. The pre-training objective follows a MLM paradigm, where 15% of genes are randomly masked, and the model is trained to predict their identities based on surrounding gene context. This approach encourages the model to learn latent expression relationships and functional gene modules, ultimately improving its capacity to distinguish cell types and phenotypes. To enhance model capacity, we developed a 24-layer variant, referred to as transformer-24L, enabling deeper representation learning and improved scalability. Moreover, rather than directly utilizing off-the-shelf pre-trained model parameters from public sources, we trained transformer-24L from scratch using our in-house compiled dataset of over 34 million single-cell profiles, offering broader diversity and improved coverage of biological states (Fig.
1B
).
  <heading level="4">Diffusion-based post-training: a denoising innovation</heading>
<![CDATA[Following the pre-training, we introduced a diffusion-based post-training stage to further refine the representations learned by transformer-24L. Specifically, we applied a discrete diffusion process to the rank-encoded transcriptomic data, aiming to enhance the expressiveness of MLM by incorporating the denoising capabilities of diffusion principles into the single-cell context. This strategy combines the strengths of both architectures: the contextual learning capacity of transformers and the distributional robustness of diffusion models. The post-training stage allows for incremental parameter updates that reduce noise and improve the fidelity of virtual cell representations within the scDifformer framework. The diffusion model leverages its inherent ability to model diversity, aligning naturally with the biological heterogeneity present in single-cell transcriptomic data. During the forward diffusion process, structured noise, representing both technical variation and biological heterogeneity, is added to the input tokens following an information-aware noise schedule. Specifically, this schedule prioritizes masking the most informative tokens early in the diffusion process, compelling the model to learn their recovery later. This mechanism aligns with the easy-first generation hypothesis common in denoising language models. The model then learned the reverse (denoising) process through a discrete diffusion framework with an absorbing state. To maintain compatibility with the pre-trained transformer model, we adopted a TAD strategy, allowing for temporal conditioning without modifying the architecture of transformer-24L. By refining the model's internal representations through post-training, this stage significantly improves the ability of scDifformer to recover biologically meaningful signals from noisy single-cell profiles (Table]]>  
1
).
