  <text>We evaluated cell annotation accuracy across seven tissues (bone marrow, PBMC, lung, pancreas, liver, kidney, and heart) using a 24-layer (24L) or a 12-layer (12L) transformer architecture, with or without the diffusion module. Reported values represent the mean accuracy across the two paired datasets per tissue. Bold values indicate the highest accuracy within each column.</text>
  <text>Crucially, both the pre- and post-training adopt a self-supervised paradigm leveraging copious amounts of unlabeled data to discern comprehensive, meaningful, and robust representations of gene expression patterns. This approach helps mitigate the labour demands of downstream fine-tuning endeavours, which typically relies on restricted volumes of annotated data. To enable scalable and efficient training on tens of millions of cells, we implemented distributed GPU training for both efficient the pre- and post-training.</text>
  <heading level="3">Diffusion post-training enhances model generalizability across studies</heading>
  <text><![CDATA[To test whether the diffusion module enhances the model's ability to reduce technical noise and better represent cellular heterogeneity, we conducted a systematic evaluation across seven tissues, bone marrow, PBMC, lung, pancreas, liver, heart, and kidney, each represented by two independent single-cell RNA-seq studies (see "Materials and methods" section). This design enabled both intra-study (within-study) and inter-study (cross-study) evaluations. While intra-study comparisons reflect the model's basic representational ability in consistent technical settings, inter-study evaluations introduce more realistic noise from batch effects, platform differences, and donor variability.]]></text>
We found that the 24-layer transformer architecture consistently outperformed its 12-layer counterpart in capturing features across datasets (
Supplementary Table S1
and Table
1
). Furthermore, it surpassed the performance of the original Geneformer model, indicating that increasing model depth improves the fidelity and generalizability of learned cell representations. We additionally evaluated the computational impact of model depth using the Kidney_Hochane/Kidney inter-dataset annotation task. Under identical hardware and training settings (4 NVIDIA A40 GPUs), the 24-layer transformer required 382 s per epoch during training on Kidney_Hochane (11 318 cells), compared to 202 s for the 12-layer model. During inference on Kidney (13 057 cells), the 24-layer model required 259.4 s, whereas the 12-layer model required 171 s. Despite the increased computational cost, the deeper architecture consistently yielded improved downstream performance and representation quality. Based on these findings, we selected the 24L transformer as the backbone for scDifformer and combined it with the diffusion module to maximize representational capacity and robustness across studies.
To evaluate the specific contribution of the diffusion module, we compared models trained with and without diffusion using the same 24-layer transformer backbone. In intra-study settings, where technical variation is minimal, the diffusion module yielded limited improvements, with gains in the lung, heart, bone marrow, and kidney datasets. However, the overall improvement trend remained consistent across tissues (
Supplementary Table S1
). In contrast, under inter-study conditions with greater technical and biological variability, the diffusion-enhanced model achieved an overall improvement in annotation accuracy across all tissues (Table
1
), underscoring its ability to generalize across heterogeneous datasets. Although the absolute gains were moderate, the consistent trends across independent datasets are in line with the hypothesis that the diffusion module may help improve the stability of latent cell representations, potentially through noise-aware refinement. However, given the current experimental design, we cannot fully disentangle the contribution of the diffusion module from other factors, and the observed improvements should not be interpreted as solely attributable to diffusion. Further evaluation across a broader range of tissues and cell types, together with additional ablation studies, would be helpful to draw more definitive conclusions.
  <text>Taken together, these results suggest that incorporating the diffusion module is associated with improved robustness and generalization across diverse datasets. This provides preliminary evidence supporting the potential value of diffusion-based refinement in single-cell modeling pipelines, particularly for settings involving heterogeneous tissues, platforms, and experimental conditions.</text>
  <heading level="3">scDifformer achieves state-of-the-art performance in cell-type annotation across diverse datasets</heading>
Cell-type annotation is a fundamental and widely used downstream task in single-cell RNA-seq data analysis. To rigorously assess performance, we constructed a standardized benchmarking framework comparing scDifformer with 10 representative annotation methods across both intra-dataset and inter-dataset settings. The compared methods include supervised classification-based approaches (scPred [
56
], scBERT [
9
], scClassify [
57
], Geneformer [
7
]), correlation-based methods (CHETAH [
58
], scmap-cell [
59
], scmap-cluster [
59
]), a marker gene-based method (Garnett [
60
]), and two foundation model-based approaches (ScFoundation and CellFM).
We first evaluated performance on six human scRNA-seq datasets (liver, kidney, lung, pancreas, bone marrow, and PMBCs). For intra-dataset benchmarking, five-fold cross-validation was performed to reduce the influence of random splits. The number of cell types per dataset ranged from 11 to 20, and total cell counts ranged from ∼8000 (typical-sized datasets) to 68 000 (large datasets). Performance was measured using accuracy and macro
  <italic>F</italic>
  <subscript>1</subscript>
<![CDATA[-score (see "Materials and methods" section). Across all datasets, scDifformer consistently outperformed all other methods on both metrics (Fig.]]>  
2A
,
Supplementary Fig. S1
, and
Supplementary Table S2
).
  <picture>
    <caption>Benchmarking cell annotation performance in intra- and inter-dataset setting. ( A, B ) Box plots showing the accuracy of eight cell annotation methods across five scRNA-seq datasets in intra-dataset (A) and inter-dataset (B) settings. For intra-dataset evaluation, five-fold cross-validation was performed within each dataset. For inter-dataset evaluation, each dataset pair was used once for training and once for testing. ( C ) Confusion matrices for kidney sample annotations in the inter-dataset setting, comparing scDifformer, Geneformer, and scBERT.</caption>
  </picture>
In real-world scenarios, users often rely on annotated reference datasets to label unannotated data [
61
]. To reflect this use case, we conducted inter-dataset benchmarking using five pairs of datasets derived from the same tissue but from different studies. Each dataset in a pair was used alternately as training and test data. scDifformer again achieved the best performance in kidney datasets and yielded comparable results to the best-performing methods in most other tissues, with the exception of pancreas (Fig.
2B
and
Supplementary Table S3
). Notably, model-based cell-type classification consistently outperforms marker gene-based approaches, highlighting the advantage of learning-based methods in handling large and complex datasets. However, a key limitation is that such models often require retraining for each tissue or cell type, reducing scalability and limiting their general applicability across diverse biological contexts.
To further investigate prediction quality, we visualized confusion matrices of the kidney dataset (Fig.
2C
and
Supplementary Fig. S2
<![CDATA[). scDifformer accurately predicted all cell types, including challenging ones with subtle differences. In contrast, scBERT failed to distinguish intercalated cell types A and B, and Geneformer showed poor performance on type B. These two cell types, both located in the kidney's collecting ducts, are involved in acid-base homeostasis []]>  
62
]. Under physiological conditions, type B cells can transdifferentiate into type A [
63
], a process essential for maintaining pH balance. Disruption of this mechanism is implicated in distal renal tubular acidosis [
64
]. The ability of scDifformer to discriminate between such functionally and transcriptionally similar cell types demonstrates its robustness and biological interpretability in real-world annotation tasks.
  <text>Together, these results demonstrate that scDifformer not only achieves state-of-the-art accuracy in cell-type annotation across a wide range of datasets but also excels in distinguishing transcriptionally similar and functionally related cell populations. Its ability to generalize across studies and tissues, combined with its strong biological interpretability, highlights its potential as a robust and reliable annotation framework for large-scale single-cell analysis.</text>
  <heading level="3">Interpreting heterogeneous cell identity through attention-guided gene and pathway analysis</heading>
<![CDATA[To explore the biological interpretability of scDifformer, we examined the attention weights from the final transformer layer to identify genes that contributed most significantly to the model's cell-type predictions (Fig.]]>  
3A
). The attention mechanism enables the model to assign context-specific importance to individual genes across the transcriptome, thereby offering interpretable insights into the molecular features driving its classification decisions.
  <picture>
    <caption>Attention-based identification of cell-type-relevant genes and functional enrichment. ( A ) Heatmaps showing log-transformed attention weights from the final transformer layer of scDifformer. The top 10 genes with the highest attention scores are shown for B cells, ciliated cells, fibroblasts, and natural killer (NK) cells from the Travaglini lung dataset. Colored columns represent the attention maps for each cell type: light blue for B cells, ochre for ciliated cells, dark blue for fibroblasts, and red for NK cells. ( B ) Bar plots of -log 10 P -values from cell-type enrichment analysis using the top 100 attention-weighted genes for each cell type. Most significantly enriched term in each case matches the true annotated identity. ( C - F ) GO enrichment of top 100 genes with the highest attention weights in each cell type (C: B cells; D: ciliated cells; E: fibroblasts; F: NK cells). Each node represents one enriched GO term; node color groups similar functional categories, node size reflects the proportion of input genes mapped to the term, and edge width indicates semantic similarity between terms.</caption>
  </picture>
We focused on four cell types, B cells, ciliated cells, fibroblasts, and NK cells, from the Travaglini lung dataset [
29
], and extracted the top 10 genes with the highest attention scores in each. In B cells, top-ranked genes included
  <italic>MS4A1</italic>
(encoding the
  <italic>CD20</italic>
protein) [
65
], a canonical B-cell marker, as well as
  <italic>CD79A</italic>
and
  <italic>CD79B</italic>
, which form part of the B cell receptor signaling complex [
66
] (light blue columns in Fig.
3A
). For ciliated cells, high-attention genes such as
  <italic>CIBAR2</italic>
and
  <italic>MAPK15</italic>
, while not traditionally considered cell-type markers, are known to be involved in ciliogenesis [
67
,
68
] (ochre columns in Fig.
3A
), suggesting the model captures relevant biological context beyond known markers. Fibroblast attention genes were strongly enriched for known markers including
  <italic>DCN, LUM, FMO2</italic>
, and
  <italic>COL1A2</italic>
[
69
,
70
] (dark blue columns in Fig.
3A
). In NK cells, the model assigned high attention to both
  <italic>IL2RB</italic>
[
71
], a surface marker, and effector molecules such as
  <italic>KLRF1</italic>
and
  <italic>FCGR3A</italic>
, involved in NK-cell-mediated cytotoxicity (red columns in Fig.
3A
).
To validate the biological relevance of these high-attention genes, we performed enrichment analysis using the top 100 attention-weighted genes for each cell type (
Supplementary Table S4
). When queried against a reference collection of human cell-type expression signatures [
51
], the most significantly enriched term in each case corresponded to the true annotated analysis further revealed strong associations with biological processes specific to each cell type (Fig.
3C
-
F
and
Supplementary Table S5
<![CDATA[). For example, B cell attention genes were enriched for "B cell activation," ciliated cell genes for "primary cilium development," fibroblast genes for "regulation of fibroblast proliferation," and NK cell genes for "natural killer cell-mediated cytotoxicity" and "granzyme-mediated programmed cell death signaling."]]>  
To further assess whether these attention-derived genes provide more reliable biological interpretation than other single-cell foundation models, we compared scDifformer with scFoundation and CellFM using the same gene-ranking and enrichment-analysis workflow. In the lung dataset, scDifformer showed a clear advantage in cell-type enrichment. As previously described, the top genes identified by scDifformer were consistently enriched for the expected cell-type signatures (Fig.
3A
). In contrast, scFoundation and CellFM produced less specific top-attention-weighted genes and enrichment profiles (
Supplementary Fig. S3
). For example, cell-cycle related gene
  <italic>BLCAP</italic>
[
72
] was identified by scFoundation for B cells and several house-keeping genes such as
  <italic>QARS1</italic>
and
  <italic>XPOT</italic>
were identified by CellFM (
Supplementary Fig. S3
). They produced unrelated epithelial, macrophage, thymocyte, neuronal, smooth muscle, pericyte, or club-cell-associated signatures among the top enriched terms (
Supplementary Fig. S4A
). These results suggest that scDifformer-derived genes better preserve the annotated cell identities in the lung dataset.
Consistently, GO enrichment analysis of the lung dataset further supported the superior functional interpretability of scDifformer. The genes prioritized by scDifformer were enriched for canonical biological programs corresponding to each cell type, including B cell activation and adaptive immune response for B cells, cilium organization and cilium movement for ciliated cells, extracellular matrix organization for fibroblasts, and immune effector/cytotoxicity-related processes for NK cells (Fig.
3C
-
F
). By contrast, scFoundation was more frequently enriched for broad metabolic or housekeeping processes, such as cellular respiration, oxidative phosphorylation, translation, and ATP biosynthetic process, whereas CellFM often yielded weakly related or mismatched pathways, including neuronal signaling, neurotransmitter transport, endoplasmic-reticulum-stress-related terms, and generic transport processes (
Supplementary Fig S4B
and
Supplementary Table S6
). Thus, in the lung dataset, scDifformer outperformed the comparison models in both cell-identity preservation and cell-type-specific functional enrichment.
To assess whether the attention patterns generalize beyond the lung dataset, we further analyzed an independent kidney sample [
36
] containing four major cell types: endothelial cells, principal cells, proximal tubule cells, and thick ascending limb cells. We extracted the top attention-weighted genes for each kidney cell type and visualized their expression patterns across cells (
Supplementary Fig. S5
). The attention heatmap showed clear cell-type-specific gene modules. Endothelial cells were characterized by high attention on vascular and endothelial-associated genes such as
  <italic>FLT1</italic>
and
  <italic>ADGRL4</italic>
[
73
]. Principal cells showed high attention on renal epithelial and collecting-duct-associated genes, including
  <italic>HSD11B2</italic>
[
74
], consistent with epithelial identity, sodium transport, and mineralocorticoid-responsive renal physiology. Proximal tubule cells were marked by reabsorption and transporter-associated genes such as
  <italic>SLC5A12</italic>
and
  <italic>ACSM2A/ACSM2B</italic>
[
75
]. Thick ascending limb cells showed high attention on canonical nephron-segment and ion-transport genes, including
  <italic>SLC12A1</italic>
[
76
] (
Supplementary Fig S5
). These gene-level patterns indicate that scDifformer attention weights capture biologically meaningful and cell-type-relevant programs in the kidney dataset rather than merely reflecting generic transcriptional activity.
We next performed enrichment analysis on the kidney attention-weighted genes using the same workflow. GO enrichment analysis showed that the scDifformer-derived genes were strongly associated with the expected functions of the corresponding renal cell types (
Supplementary Fig. S6A and B
;
Supplementary Table S6
). Endothelial cells were enriched for vascular programs such as angiogenesis, blood vessel morphogenesis, vasculature development, and tube morphogenesis. Principal cells were enriched for renal system development, kidney development, intracellular chemical homeostasis, and ion homeostasis. Proximal tubule cells showed enrichment for membrane localization and ion transport, including protein localization to membrane, metal ion transport, and monoatomic cation transmembrane transport. Thick ascending limb cells were enriched for metal ion transport and ion-homeostasis-related pathways, consistent with the known role of this nephron segment in electrolyte transport. These results demonstrate that scDifformer attention-derived genes remain functionally interpretable in an independent tissue context.
We also compared the kidney enrichment results with scFoundation and CellFM (
Supplementary Fig. S6A and B
;
Supplementary Table S6
). Unlike the lung dataset, cell-type enrichment results in the kidney sample were less discriminative among the three models, likely because multiple renal tubular cell types share epithelial lineage and overlapping kidney epithelial signatures. As expected, GO enrichment revealed a clear advantage of scDifformer. While scDifformer recovered renal cell-type-relevant functional programs, scFoundation and CellFM were more frequently associated with broader or less cell-type-specific processes, such as RNA splicing, protein folding, nucleotide metabolism, cellular respiration, chromatin remodeling, and generic metabolic programs. These findings indicate that, even when broad cell-type signature enrichment is comparable, scDifformer identifies genes that more accurately reflect the functional programs of kidney endothelial and tubular epithelial cells.
  <text>These findings demonstrate that scDifformer assigns biologically meaningful attention to cell-type-relevant genes, underscoring its potential not only as a high-performing classifier but also as a tool for interpretable discovery of molecular programs that define cellular identity.</text>
  <heading level="3">Tracing immune cell development across tissues through embedding-guided trajectory inference</heading>
Building on the gene marker and pathway-level interpretability demonstrated in heterogeneous immune cell populations, we next investigated whether the cell embeddings learned by scDifformer could capture developmental trajectories across distinct immune tissues. To this end, we curated scRNA-seq datasets from bone marrow [
77
], spleen [
77
], thymus [
78
], and PBMCs [
79
], encompassing immune cells spanning hematopoietic stem and HSPCs to terminally differentiated lineages. We first compared the performance of scDifformer embeddings against a widely used reference model, scVI [
80
], scFoundation [
12
], and scGPT [
10
], to assess whether our embeddings could preserve both cell type identity and tissue-specific information. We then employed Slingshot [
81
] for pseudotime inference, using cluster-level connectivity derived from Partition-based Graph Abstraction [
82
] to reconstruct cross-tissue lineage maps.
Visualization of the cell embeddings projected with UMAP showed that raw expression data (Fig.
4A
) suffered from strong batch effects and poor cell type separation. In contrast, embeddings learned from scVI (Fig.
4B
), scDifformer (Fig.
4C
), scFoundation (Fig.
4D
), and scGPT (Fig.
4E
) significantly improved cellular resolution. Notably, scDifformer generated more compact and continuous clusters, better reflecting underlying developmental transitions. To quantitatively evaluate the inferred trajectories, we adopted five criteria: biological plausibility, directional coherence, confidence distribution, lineage continuity, and topological connectivity. Canonical hematopoietic hierarchies served as references for biological validity, where HSPCs differentiate into three major lineages [
83
,
84
]: (i) lymphoid (e.g. B cells, T cells, NK cells), (ii) myeloid (e.g. granulocytes, monocytes, dendritic cells), and (iii) erythroid/megakaryocyte lineages. Trajectory inference using scDifformer embeddings (Fig.
4C
) recovered multiple well-supported developmental paths, including HSPC → megakaryocyte and monocyte → dendritic cell differentiation, with clear directional transitions from progenitors to mature immune cell types. In contrast, the scVI-based trajectories (Fig.
4B
<![CDATA[) mainly captured lateral transitions between immune subtypes (e.g. CD4 to CD8 T cells), and even biologically implausible paths such as megakaryocyte → HSPC. Though both models exhibited relatively low global confidence scores (mean < 0.05), key transitions in the scDifformer map, such as monocyte → dendritic cell (confidence = 0.09), received stronger support. Notably, scDifformer also identified a transition from CD4 to CD8 T cells. While this conversion is not part of the canonical T cell differentiation hierarchy, it has been reported under specific pathological contexts such as chronic inflammation or viral infection []]>  
85
], where T cells may exhibit phenotypic plasticity or reprogramming. Although our benchmarking datasets were not derived from these disease states, it is possible that the pretraining corpus included related immune profiles, enabling the model to learn such rare transitions. For scFoundation, the inferred trajectories show limited biological consistency. Several reversed lineage relationships were observed (e.g. B cell → HSPC, dendritic cell → HSPC), which contradict established hematopoietic hierarchies. In addition, T, NK, and ILC populations are poorly resolved, suggesting the embeddings capture transcriptional similarity rather than developmental structure. Overall, these embeddings are less suitable for trajectory inference under the same framework. For scGPT, the inferred structure resembles a transcriptional similarity graph rather than a lineage trajectory. We observed multiple cross-lineage and reversed connections (e.g. B cell → macrophage, mast cell → HSPC), along with extensive mixing among T, NK, and ILC populations. This suggests the embeddings prioritize expression similarity over developmental directionality. Overall, considering both biological plausibility and global coherence of the inferred trajectories, scDifformer performs best in trajectory inference. The model demonstrates strong sensitivity in capturing subtle or underrepresented cell state transitions. While most inferred trajectories are consistent with known biology, a small number of implausible connections (e.g. macrophage → megakaryocyte) are observed, likely reflecting residual technical noise or integration artifacts.
  <picture>
    <caption>Comparison of scDifformer and the other models embeddings for immune cell resolution and developmental trajectory inference. ( A ) UMAP projection of unintegrated single-cell gene expression profiles, colored by cell type (top) and tissue of origin (bottom). The embedding shows pronounced batch effects and poor separation among cell types. ( B ) UMAP and Slingshot-inferred pseudotime trajectories based on scVI embeddings. While scVI reduces batch effects, the resulting trajectories are sparse and exhibit limited biological directionality. ( C ) UMAP and pseudotime trajectories derived from scDifformer embeddings. Cells of the same type form compact, continuous clusters with clear lineage transitions from HSPCs to differentiated immune subsets across bone marrow, spleen, thymus, and peripheral blood. Compared to scVI, scDifformer better preserves developmental continuity and aligns more closely with canonical hematopoietic hierarchies. ( D ) UMAP and Slingshot-inferred pseudotime trajectories based on scFoundation embeddings. ( E ) UMAP and Slingshot-inferred pseudotime trajectories based on scGPT embeddings.</caption>
  </picture>
  <text><![CDATA[These results demonstrate that scDifformer's context-aware embeddings support more accurate and biologically coherent pseudotime inference than traditional approaches. The ability to reconstruct lineage hierarchies across diverse tissues highlights its potential for modeling dynamic cell state transitions, reinforcing its role as a core component in building virtual immune cell representations.]]></text>
  <heading level="3">Cross-modal application of scDifformer to spatial spot deconvolution</heading>
  <text>To extend the application of scDifformer embeddings beyond conventional transcriptomic analyses, we investigated their utility in spatial transcriptomics deconvolution, a task that aims to infer the underlying cell-type composition of spatial spots. Spatial deconvolution offers a potential avenue for bridging single-cell and spatial transcriptomic modalities by leveraging the cell-type information learned from scRNA-seq to interpret spatial data. Given the performance of scDifformer embeddings in cell annotation, we hypothesized that they could also enhance spatial deconvolution accuracy.</text>
We integrated scDifformer-derived embeddings with a graph neural network, GraphSAGE [
46
], creating a hybrid GraphSAGE-scDifformer model (Fig.
5A
<![CDATA[; see "Materials and methods" section). To benchmark its performance, we generated simulated spatial transcriptomics (ST) data by gridding cell coordinates from a spatially resolved single-cell dataset []]>  
43
], resulting in 253 artificial spots with known cell type compositions (Fig.
5B
). We compared our model against seven established deconvolution methods spanning multiple algorithmic categories: probabilistic (RCTD [
86
], STRIDE [
87
], Cell2location [
88
]), deep learning (Tangram [
89
]), non-negative matrix factorization (SpatialDWLS [
90
]), graph-based approaches (DSTG [
91
], stdGCN [
45
]), and a spatial transcriptomics foundation model-based approach (scGPT-spatial [
92
]).
  <picture>
    <caption>scDifformer in spatial transcriptome deconvolution. ( A ) Schematic overview of scDifformer-GraphSAGE for deconvolution of spatial transcriptomics data. We first randomly generated the pseudo-ST data with cell mixtures from scRNA-seq data. Based on the pseudo- and real-spots, we find the k -nearest neighbors for each spot using the expression matrix and construct a link graph containing all the spots. Based on the link graph, the graph convolutional network is utilized to propagate both pseudo- and real-spots data into the latent layer and discern the compositions of distinct cell types in each spot. The cell embeddings extracted from scDiffomer will be concatenated with the spot expression matrix as the input of GraphSAGE module. Finally, we get the spot-cell-type matrix containing the proportion of each cell type in each spot. ( B ) A spatially resolved single-cell transcriptome dataset from mouse cortex. Each dot represents a single cell, and colors represent different cell types. We grided these cells based on their coordinates to generate spots with known cell type composition. ( C, D ) Radar charts showing the ranking of deconvolution performance of different methods measured by PCC, SSIM, RMSE, and JSD. The outermost layer of a radar chart represents the best performance, including the highest PCC/SSIM and the lowest JSD/RMSE. Gridded spots of single-cell level mouse cortex sample (C) and simulated spatial transcriptome data from scRNA-seq of Human pancreas data (D) are used as test samples.</caption>
  </picture>
Performance was assessed using four complementary metrics: JSD, PCC, RMSE, and SSIM. GraphSAGE-scDifformer achieved the best scores in JSD (0.44), RMSE (0.15), and SSIM (0.57) (Fig.
5C
and
Supplementary Fig. S7
), indicating accurate reconstruction of spatial compositions. Notably, GraphSAGE-scDifformer also outperformed the specialized spatial transcriptomics foundation model scGPT-spatial in both spatial pattern identification and deconvolution accuracy (
Supplementary Table S9
), further demonstrating the robustness and generalization capability of our framework for spatial transcriptomics analysis. To further validate generalizability, we conducted an additional benchmark on pancreas data using simulated spots generated via random sampling of cells. Our model outperformed the top three deconvolution tools from a prior benchmark (Fig.
5D
) [
42
]. To isolate the contribution of the scDifformer embeddings, we compared the original GraphSAGE with the GraphSAGE-scDifformer variant. Across all four metrics, the integration of diffusion-derived embeddings led to significant improvements (Wilcoxon test,
  <italic>P</italic>
<![CDATA[< .05;]]>  
Supplementary Fig. S8
), highlighting the utility of pretrained single-cell embeddings in spatial modeling tasks.
  <text>In summary, our results demonstrate that scDifformer embeddings, when integrated with a graph neural network framework (GraphSAGE), provide a powerful tool for improving spatial transcriptomics deconvolution. By leveraging the rich, context-aware representations learned from large-scale single-cell data, GraphSAGE-scDifformer significantly enhances the accuracy of cell type composition inference in spatial spots. Our model not only outperforms existing deconvolution methods across multiple performance metrics but also offers a novel approach for bridging the gap between single-cell and spatial transcriptomics, enabling more precise spatial resolution of cellular identities. These findings underscore the potential of scDifformer as a versatile model for both cell identity annotation and dynamic cellular modeling in spatially resolved data.</text>
  <heading level="2">Discussion</heading>
  <text>In this study, we developed scDifformer, a large-scale single-cell foundation model that integrates the transformer-based architecture of Geneformer with a diffusion probabilistic module via a novel post-training step. Leveraging an extensive pre-training corpus of 34.2 million human single-cell transcriptomes from publicly available databases including DISCO and CZ CELLxGENE, scDifformer surpasses existing models such as scBERT and Geneformer, both in scale and downstream annotation performance. The diffusion-enhanced architecture effectively reduces data-driven noise, refines learned representations, and accelerates convergence. Moreover, our attention-based interpretation revealed biologically meaningful gene signatures, linking top-ranked attention genes to enriched pathways consistent with known cell identities. Additionally, extending scDifformer embeddings to spatial transcriptomics through integration with the GraphSAGE graph neural network demonstrated superior performance in simulated spot-level deconvolution tasks. This innovative step represents the first successful application of diffusion modeling in spatial transcriptomics, suggesting a promising strategy for future integration of spatial and single-cell data.</text>
  <text><![CDATA[Despite these advances, several limitations must be acknowledged. First, our current dataset primarily includes normal or steady-state cellular profiles, with limited representation of abnormal, perturbed, or disease-associated cells. This restricts the model's predictive capacity in pathological conditions, limiting its immediate applicability in areas such as disease modeling or therapeutic perturbation studies. Second, while our cross-tissue analysis validated performance using immune cells, a relatively well-defined cellular population, future studies should investigate more complex developmental processes, such as embryonic development or stem-cell differentiation pathways. Modeling these processes could substantially impact developmental biology and regenerative medicine, including advancements in artificial organ technologies. Third, although we demonstrated potential for integrating single-cell and spatial transcriptomics data, scDifformer is not inherently a multi-modal model. Comprehensive multi-modal frameworks should integrate additional data types, such as proteomics, epigenomics, and metabolomics, to fully capture cellular complexity and interactions at multiple molecular layers.]]></text>
  <text><![CDATA[Addressing these limitations will guide our future research efforts. One critical direction is to expand training datasets to encompass diverse abnormal or perturbed cellular states, enhancing the model's ability to capture disease progression, cellular response to treatments, and dynamic reprogramming events. Additionally, the development of fully multi-modal foundation models, capable of jointly embedding transcriptomic, proteomic, and other molecular data, represents an exciting avenue to build next-generation virtual cells. Such models would offer deeper insights into cellular function and regulation, significantly advancing our capability to reconstruct accurate, context-aware biological simulations.]]></text>
